Build and publish the site from Actions, not Cloudflare's git app

Cloudflare's git integration stopped deploying and began reporting the
repository as damaged. This moves the publish step into the workflow that was
already building the site, so the thing that broke is no longer in the path.

## What changed

`.github/workflows/deploy.yml` gains a Cloudflare deploy after the existing
build: `npx wrangler deploy`, on push to `main` only. It reads

| What | Where | Value |
|---|---|---|
| `CLOUDFLARE_PAGES_TOKEN` | org **secret** | API token, *Edit Cloudflare Workers* template |
| `CLOUDFLARE_PAGES_PROJECT` | repo **variable** | `fcpm` |
| `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` | repo **variable**, optional | only if the token sees more than one account |

and skips with a step-summary message when the token is absent, the same way
the Azure and broker deploys already do. **This can merge before the secret
exists** and CI stays green.

Azure is untouched — still wired, still skipped, still dormant.

## Why the git integration probably broke, which I want on the record

Not certain, but it fits everything I can see. `.advocate-engine` was mounted as
a submodule on 2026-09-04, pinned to a commit that is **not at the tip of a
branch**. Cloudflare's builder clones shallow and then initialises submodules —
the one combination that cannot fetch such a commit. A superproject whose
submodule will not resolve is fairly called damaged.

**The asymmetry is the evidence.** CI has been green the whole time, because
`actions/checkout` does not fetch submodules unless asked and nothing in
`_site` comes from `.advocate-engine`. Cloudflare failed for exactly the reason
Actions did not.

So there is a note in the workflow and in the README: **do not add
`submodules: true` to that checkout.** It would import the failure and buy
nothing.

## The decision I made without asking, and how to reverse it

You asked for Pages names, and I used them — but the deploy underneath is
`wrangler deploy`, not `wrangler pages deploy`.

This project is a **Worker with static assets**, not a classic Pages project;
the README has said so since the Cloudflare section was written. `wrangler
pages deploy` would not have replaced the live site. It would have created a
**second** one, at its own address, with the custom domain still pointing at
the first — two live copies of the website, which is the thing the vendors
advocate spent its first session warning about.

If you actually want to migrate to a classic Pages project, that is a real
migration with a DNS change at the end of it, and it should be its own decision
rather than a side effect of fixing a broken build. Say so and I will do it
properly.

The secret and variable keep the `…_PAGES_…` names regardless. They are ours,
they describe the static host in the words we use for it, and renaming them
later is a settings change.

## What this loses

Pull request preview URLs. Cloudflare's integration gave every PR one; this
does not, because there is one live copy and a PR must not become it.

`wrangler versions upload` is how they come back — it uploads a version and
prints its URL without promoting it. I left it out deliberately rather than
half-building it: it needs the token to carry more permission, and it is a
choice, not an oversight.

## Verified

- The workflow parses (`YAML.load_file`), and the steps are in the intended
  order: build → Cloudflare → Azure.
- The build step is unchanged, so the pull request gate is unchanged.
- Nothing here touches the broker, `worker/`, or any Stripe path.

## Not done

- The secret and both variables. Those are yours; nothing here can create them.
- Whether to disconnect the Cloudflare git app. **Do that when this merges** —
  until you do, Cloudflare will keep trying and keep failing, and once the
  token is in you would have two things deploying the same site.
